(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-cms-exam-3-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 3 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 54-year-old has a painless, slowly enlarging 1.5 cm firm swelling of the hard palate, present for eight months. There is no pain, no facial weakness and no cervical lymphadenopathy. A friend whose parotid lump proved benign has told the patient not to worry. What should this patient be told about how the site affects the risk of malignancy?","choices":["Only the parotid gives rise to malignant tumors","Site has no bearing on the likelihood of malignancy","The larger the gland, the higher the proportion that are malignant","The smaller the gland, the higher the proportion that are malignant"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Parotid tumors are 75 to 80 percent benign, submandibular 50 to 60 percent, and minor salivary glands only about 35 percent benign. The relationship runs inversely with gland size, which is a memorable rule and changes how aggressively a small-gland lesion is investigated. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 139"},{"q":"An audiogram of a 45-year-old factory worker shows a notch at 4000 hertz with recovery at 8000 hertz. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Meniere disease","Noise-induced hearing loss","Ototoxicity","Presbycusis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cumulative noise exposure classically produces a notch centered around 4000 hertz with some recovery at higher frequencies, which is what distinguishes it from presbycusis. Presbycusis declines progressively as frequency rises and does not recover, so the shape of the curve rather than its depth makes the diagnosis. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man needs wax removed. He has a history of a tympanic membrane perforation repaired years ago. What is the most appropriate method of removal?","choices":["Cotton bud removal by the patient at home","Microsuction or curette under direct vision","Softening drops alone for two weeks","Warm water irrigation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Irrigation is contraindicated when the drum may not be intact, because water forced into the middle ear seeds it with canal organisms and causes a suppurative otitis media. Removing the wax under direct vision keeps the procedure entirely within the canal and lets the operator see the drum as it is uncovered. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 58-year-old smoker is found to have a 3 cm cervical node on a routine visit. It has been present for about six weeks. There has been no fever, sore throat or recent illness, and no weight loss. The clinician is deciding whether this warrants urgent investigation. Which features on palpation would most suggest malignancy?","choices":["Bilateral distribution","Immobility and non-tenderness","Softness and fluctuance","Tenderness and rapid enlargement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The descriptors that matter are: size, shape, mobility, consistency and tenderness. An immobile node suggests fixation to surrounding structures by tumor, and non-tenderness points away from an inflammatory cause, since inflamed nodes hurt. Persistence or continued enlargement is what triggers fine needle aspiration. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 27-year-old presents with a firm, painless 2 cm lateral neck mass that has been present for two months. There is no fever, sore throat or recent infection, and no night sweats or weight loss. Examination is otherwise unremarkable. Before any test is ordered, what is the most likely nature of this mass?","choices":["Above 40 most masses are congenital","Age is not a useful discriminator in neck masses","Under 40 most are inflammatory, except Hodgkin lymphoma","Under 40 most masses are malignant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most neck masses in patients under 40 are inflammatory in nature, and names Hodgkin lymphoma as the specific exception to that rule. Above 40 the probability shifts toward malignancy, which is why age over 40 appears on the red-flag list alongside size, duration, firmness and tobacco use. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with poorly controlled diabetes has facial pain, black necrotic tissue visible on the nasal turbinate, and rapidly progressive periorbital swelling. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A fungus ball in the maxillary sinus","Acute bacterial sinusitis with orbital extension","Allergic fungal sinusitis","Invasive fungal sinusitis, needing debridement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Black necrotic mucosa in an immunocompromised or diabetic patient indicates angioinvasive fungal disease, where hyphae invade blood vessels and cause tissue infarction. The blood supply is destroyed, so antifungal drugs alone cannot reach the dead tissue; surgical debridement is required alongside systemic therapy and control of the diabetes. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 10-year-old has a two-day sore throat with a temperature of 101 degrees Fahrenheit, tonsillar exudate and tender anterior cervical nodes. She has no cough. A rapid antigen detection test is negative. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Accept the negative test and treat symptomatically","Order antistreptolysin O titres to make the diagnosis today","Send a throat culture before deciding about antibiotics","Start penicillin immediately without further testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Her Centor score is high, with a point each for absence of cough, exudate, fever and tender anterior nodes, plus one for her age. Rapid antigen tests are specific but not sufficiently sensitive, so a negative result in a child with a high clinical probability is always confirmed by culture rather than accepted. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman describes a whooshing sound in her right ear that keeps time with her pulse. It has been present for two months and is louder when she lies down. Her hearing is subjectively normal. How should this symptom be characterized?","choices":["A symptom of Meniere disease","An expected consequence of presbycusis","Ordinary subjective tinnitus requiring only reassurance and sound therapy","Pulsatile unilateral tinnitus, which requires investigation for a vascular cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most tinnitus is a non-pulsatile bilateral ringing that accompanies sensorineural loss and needs no imaging. Tinnitus that is unilateral or synchronous with the pulse is the red flag combination, because it suggests turbulent blood flow or a vascular middle ear lesion such as a glomus tumor, and those need to be found. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 5-year-old has chronic mouth breathing, snoring and hyponasal speech with bilateral nasal obstruction. There is no discharge. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Adenoidal hypertrophy","Deviated septum","Nasal foreign body","Nasal polyposis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The adenoid sits in the nasopharynx behind the nasal cavity, so enlargement obstructs both sides equally and produces mouth breathing, snoring and the hyponasal voice of a blocked nose. The bilateral, discharge-free pattern is what distinguishes it from a foreign body or infection. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has had gradually worsening hearing over three years, worse since her pregnancy. She says she hears conversation better in a noisy restaurant than in a quiet room. Otoscopy is normal. Her father had a similar problem. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Otitis media with effusion","Otosclerosis","Presbycusis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hearing better in background noise, called paracusis, happens because a conductive loss filters out low-frequency ambient noise more than speech, and because people raise their voices in noisy rooms. Combined with a normal drum, a family history, worsening in pregnancy, and gradual conductive loss, this is the classic picture of stapes fixation. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 34-year-old has had nasal congestion, purulent discharge and facial pressure for five weeks. The symptoms began after a cold, improved briefly, and have persisted since. There is no fever today. The clinician is documenting the diagnosis and needs to classify it by duration. How are acute, subacute and chronic rhinosinusitis separated?","choices":["Acute under four weeks, chronic beyond twelve weeks","Acute under one week, chronic beyond four weeks","Acute under twelve weeks, chronic beyond six months","Duration is not used to classify rhinosinusitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute disease resolves within four weeks, subacute occupies the interval between four and twelve, and chronic is defined by symptoms persisting beyond twelve weeks. The boundaries matter because the mechanism shifts from a single infection to impaired mucociliary clearance with persistent inflammation, and so does the treatment. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 12"},{"q":"An 8-year-old boy has left ear pain. His father says he has been swimming almost daily in a neighbor's pool this summer. Examination shows erythema and edema of the left ear canal and pain on manipulation of the pinna. The tympanic membrane is not well visualized but appears intact, and the right ear is normal. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Oral amoxicillin suspension","Topical ciprofloxacin-dexamethasone","Topical clotrimazole 1%","Topical triamcinolone 0.1%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pain on moving the pinna or tragus localizes the disease to the CANAL, not the middle ear, and water exposure macerates the canal skin and raises its pH so that Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus overgrow. Treatment therefore has to reach the canal surface: a topical fluoroquinolone covers those organisms, and the added steroid shrinks the edema so the drops can actually get past it. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A newborn fails the hearing screen on both ears before discharge. On examination there is a white forelock, widely spaced medial canthi and heterochromia of the irises. The pregnancy and delivery were uncomplicated and there was no known infection. A parent has a similar white forelock. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Alport syndrome","Nonsyndromic hereditary hearing loss","Usher syndrome","Waardenburg syndrome"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Waardenburg is a neural crest disorder, and the neural crest contributes both to the melanocytes of the skin, hair and iris and to the stria vascularis of the cochlea. That shared origin is why pigmentary abnormalities and sensorineural deafness appear together, and it is the syndrome the described features define. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 9-year-old has a 2-day history of left ear pain. The canal is too swollen and tender to admit the speculum, so the drum cannot be seen, and the clinician cannot say whether the problem is in the canal or the middle ear. Temperature is 98.9 degrees Fahrenheit. Which bedside maneuver best separates the two?","choices":["Check for fever","Look for discharge in the canal","Move the tragus or pinna","Perform a Weber test"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pain on manipulating the pinna or tragus localizes the disease to the canal, because moving those structures moves the inflamed canal skin. Middle ear infection sits behind an intact drum and is unaffected by moving the external ear, so the maneuver separates the two before the otoscope is even used. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman is struck on the side of the head with an open hand. She has immediate ear pain, a brief episode of bleeding from the canal, and reduced hearing. Otoscopy shows a small central perforation with clean edges. There is no vertigo and the facial nerve is intact. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Irrigate the canal to remove blood clot","Keep the ear dry and review","Refer for immediate tympanoplasty","Start oral antibiotics for all traumatic perforations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A small traumatic perforation with clean edges and no vertigo or facial nerve deficit implies the ossicular chain and inner ear are intact. The drum has good spontaneous healing capacity, so the treatment is to protect it: keep water out so bacteria do not reach the middle ear through the hole, and allow it to close on its own. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man with untreated HIV has a rapidly enlarging cervical mass with purple discoloration of the overlying skin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Idiopathic follicular hyperplasia","Kaposi sarcoma","Persistent generalized lymphadenopathy","Reactive viral lymphadenopathy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Kaposi sarcoma is one of the conditions that must be excluded in an HIV-positive patient with lymphadenopathy, alongside tuberculosis, Pneumocystis and lymphoma. Its vascular nature produces the characteristic purple appearance, which distinguishes it from the follicular hyperplasia that causes most adenopathy in this group. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has a bead in the left nostril. The child is calm and the object is visible anteriorly. What is the most appropriate first attempt at removal?","choices":["A parent-delivered positive pressure blow","Grasp the bead with forceps","Irrigate the nostril with saline under pressure","Refer immediately for removal under general anesthesia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The parent seals the child's mouth with their own and blows while the unaffected nostril is occluded, so the pressure travels through the posterior choana and pushes the object forward. It is atraumatic, needs no instruments, and avoids the risk of pushing the bead deeper that instrumentation carries in a small child. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has severe sore throat, odynophagia and a muffled voice for a day. He is febrile but not drooling and has no stridor. Fiberoptic laryngoscopy shows a swollen epiglottis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Admit for observation, intravenous antibiotics and airway monitoring","Discharge on oral antibiotics with review in 24 hours","Immediate tracheostomy","Lateral neck radiography before any treatment"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adult supraglottitis progresses more slowly than the pediatric form and often does not require immediate intubation, so observation with humidification, glucocorticoids, intravenous antibiotics and nebulized adrenaline is appropriate. The airway is monitored because intubation or tracheostomy becomes necessary if it obstructs. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man of southern Chinese origin has a firm neck mass, intermittent double vision and numbness of the left cheek, with unilateral nasal obstruction and occasional blood-stained nasal discharge. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis","Chronic bacterial sinusitis","Nasal polyposis","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The combination of a neck mass, cranial nerve involvement giving diplopia and facial numbness, and unilateral nasal symptoms is the described presentation. The tumor sits in the nasopharynx where it drains early to cervical nodes and can invade the skull base, and it is strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus and endemic in southern China. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 5-year-old girl has had a foul-smelling discharge from the left ear for a week. She has been afebrile with no ear pain. Otoscopy after suctioning reveals a small piece of foam rubber impacted against the drum. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Chronic suppurative otitis media","Malignant otitis externa","Otomycosis","Retained foreign body with infection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An organic or porous object retained in the canal obstructs drainage and acts as a nidus, so the canal skin becomes secondarily infected and produces the foul discharge. The absence of pain and fever, with a unilateral offensive discharge in a young child, is the pattern that should prompt a careful search for something in the canal. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 47"},{"q":"An 81-year-old woman has a rapidly enlarging hard thyroid mass with hoarseness and difficulty swallowing that has developed over six weeks. Biopsy shows undifferentiated giant and spindle cells. What is the diagnosis, and what is the prognosis?","choices":["Anaplastic carcinoma, with a prognosis measured in months","Medullary carcinoma","Papillary carcinoma, with an excellent prognosis","Primary thyroid lymphoma arising in Hashimoto thyroiditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anaplastic carcinoma is about 1 percent of thyroid cancers, occurs in elderly patients, and is composed of small cell, giant cell and spindle cell types. It is the most aggressive form, typically causing death in 6 to 36 months, and is resistant to all treatment modalities, which is why the rapid growth and compressive symptoms are characteristic. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man attends a loud concert and afterwards notices muffled hearing and ringing in both ears. By two days later his hearing has returned to normal, though the ringing lingers briefly. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A temporary threshold shift, which recovers within 24 to 48 hours","Acoustic trauma from a single impulse noise","Early presbycusis unmasked by the noise","Sudden sensorineural hearing loss requiring urgent referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intense sound temporarily fatigues the cochlear hair cells, raising the threshold at which sound is detected, and function returns as the cells recover over 24 to 48 hours. The clinical importance is that repeated temporary shifts eventually become permanent, so the recovery is a warning rather than a reassurance. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 3-year-old treated for acute otitis media returns on day six with worsening ear pain, fever, and redness and tenderness behind the ear. The auricle is pushed forward and out. The parent asks why an ear infection spread to the bone behind the ear rather than to the sinuses. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Infection spreads hematogenously to the mastoid","The eustachian tube drains into the mastoid","The mastoid air cells communicate directly with the middle ear","The mastoid shares a wall with the paranasal sinuses"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mastoid antrum opens into the epitympanum through the aditus, so the mastoid air cells and the middle ear form one continuous mucosa-lined space. Infection therefore extends into the mastoid by direct continuity, which is why mastoiditis is the classic suppurative complication of untreated otitis media. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man has a painless, fluctuant swelling in the submandibular region that has been slowly enlarging. Biopsy shows filamentous organisms. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Amphotericin B","Antituberculous therapy","Penicillin","Surgical excision alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Actinomycosis presents as a painless fluctuant mass in the submandibular or upper digastric region, and the combination of painlessness with fluctuance is what distinguishes it from an ordinary pyogenic abscess, which would be exquisitely tender. Diagnosis is clinical and by biopsy, and penicillin is the treatment. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 34"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with chronic rhinosinusitis is told the underlying problem is impaired mucociliary clearance. He asks what that means. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["The cilia can no longer move the mucus blanket out of the sinus","The immune system no longer produces antibodies to sinus organisms","The mucus has become too thin to trap organisms","The sinus ostium has been surgically closed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sinus mucosa is lined with ciliated epithelium that sweeps a mucus layer toward the ostium in a fixed direction. Chronic inflammation damages the cilia and thickens the mucus, so secretions stagnate in the sinus, which perpetuates inflammation and invites repeated infection. That is why chronic disease behaves differently from a single acute infection. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with otosclerosis asks about the risk to her children. What should the patient be told?","choices":["It has no hereditary component at all","It is caused by recurrent middle ear infection in childhood","It is purely acquired from noise exposure","It often runs in families and may worsen during pregnancy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Otosclerosis has a strong familial tendency, which is why a family history supports the diagnosis. Progression during pregnancy is also recognized, which is relevant counseling for a woman of childbearing age who is deciding when to have surgery. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man who smokes heavily and uses his voice loudly at work has six months of hoarseness. Laryngoscopy shows a single pedunculated lesion on the left vocal fold with visible vascular markings. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A course of oral corticosteroids","Microlaryngoscopic excision","Observation with repeat laryngoscopy in a year","Speech therapy alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A polyp is unilateral and pedunculated, which distinguishes it from bilateral symmetric nodules, and it is commoner in men who smoke and abuse the voice. Excision both treats it and provides tissue, which matters because a large polyp may hide an occult early laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma in exactly this risk group. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 47-year-old has had gradual hearing loss in both ears over several years, worse in the right. There is no pain, discharge or vertigo. Otoscopy is normal and the canals are clear. Tympanometry shows normal middle ear pressure with an abnormally shallow peak. What does this tympanogram indicate?","choices":["A stiff mechanism, as in ossicular fixation","Eustachian tube dysfunction","Fluid in the middle ear","Ossicular discontinuity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peak compliance occurring at normal pressure means the middle ear is ventilating properly, so the eustachian tube is working. A shallow peak means the system does not move much at any pressure, which indicates stiffness rather than fluid. That is the type As pattern and points to ossicular fixation such as otosclerosis, or to tympanosclerosis. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 19-year-old student has recurrent painful oral ulcers with yellow-gray centers and red halos, each under a centimeter, appearing on the inner cheek and inner lip and healing in about a week. They recur during examination periods. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Herpetic gingivostomatitis","Herpetiform aphthous ulceration","Major aphthous ulceration","Minor aphthous ulceration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Minor aphthae are under a centimeter, are the commonest form, burn and tingle before they appear, and heal in 7 to 10 days without scarring. Their location is diagnostic: they occur on freely moving, NON-keratinized mucosa such as buccal and labial mucosa, and stress is a recognized precipitant. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 24-year-old has a 3 cm anterior neck swelling that appeared over about ten days and is mildly tender. There has been a low-grade fever. On palpation the mass is soft and gives the sensation of a fluid wave under the fingers. What does this finding most suggest?","choices":["A cystic lesion","A lipoma","A metastatic lymph node","A vascular lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fluctuance means the mass transmits pressure as a fluid would, so it contains liquid rather than solid tissue. That is one of the four rules of thumb, alongside pulsatility indicating vascular, years of stability indicating benign, and rapid growth indicating infection or lymphoma. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has a squamous cell carcinoma of the tonsil that is human papillomavirus positive. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Chemotherapy alone as first-line treatment","Observation, since human papillomavirus related tumors regress","Surgical resection alone","Surgical resection with radiotherapy, which gives better functional outcomes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oropharyngeal tumors are treated with resection plus radiotherapy, and radiotherapy gives better functional outcomes in this site, where speech and swallowing are at stake. Between 60 and 80 percent of oropharyngeal cancers are human papillomavirus related, which is also relevant to prognosis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 7-year-old has a unilateral, solitary, tender anterior cervical node following a sore throat. Which organism is most likely responsible?","choices":["Bartonella henselae","Beta-hemolytic streptococcus, which accounts for about 70 percent","Mycobacterium tuberculosis","Staphylococcus aureus, which accounts for the majority"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Local infections of the ear, nose and throat drain to regional nodes, and about 70 percent of these unilateral solitary anterior cervical nodes relate to beta-hemolytic streptococcal infection. Around 20 percent are staphylococcal including MRSA, and 10 percent are viral, atypical mycobacterial or Bartonella. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 52-year-old professional trumpet player has hoarseness, cough and a sensation of a foreign body in the throat. Laryngoscopy shows a smooth dilation at the level of the false cord. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Laryngeal carcinoma","Laryngocele","Vocal cord papillomatosis","Vocal cord polyp"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A laryngocele is abnormal dilation or herniation of the laryngeal saccule, and sustained raised intraluminal pressure from wind instrument playing is a classic association. The smooth dilation at the level of the false cord on laryngoscopy is the characteristic finding, and computed tomography confirms the diagnosis and defines its extent. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is brought by her husband, who says she never listens to him. She admits she sometimes asks people on her right to repeat themselves. She denies dizziness, headache and visual change. Her only medication is furosemide. Weber lateralizes to the left. On the left, air conduction lasts 15 seconds and bone conduction 10 seconds; on the right, air conduction lasts 22 seconds and bone conduction 10 seconds. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["Cerumen impaction","Meniere disease","Otosclerosis","Ototoxicity from the diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both ears have air conduction longer than bone conduction, which is the normal Rinne pattern and is also what a sensorineural ear gives, so the loss must be sensorineural rather than conductive. Weber lateralizing to the left means the RIGHT is the worse ear, matching her symptom. Loop diuretics are ototoxic, and that is the only exposure in the history. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man is struck on the ear with an open hand and has immediate pain, hearing loss and vertigo, with blood in the canal. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Assess urgently for inner ear injury as well as a perforation","Irrigate the canal to clear the blood","Prescribe vestibular suppressants and discharge","Reassure and review in two weeks, as most perforations heal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vertigo after ear trauma is the finding that changes the picture. A simple perforation does not cause vertigo, so its presence raises the possibility of ossicular disruption with a perilymphatic fistula or direct labyrinthine injury, which needs prompt specialist assessment rather than routine review. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has a breathy hoarse voice and occasional coughing while drinking, six weeks after thyroidectomy. Laryngoscopy shows one immobile vocal fold. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bilateral vocal cord paralysis","Laryngeal edema from intubation","Unilateral paralysis from recurrent laryngeal injury","Vocal cord nodules from postoperative voice strain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The recurrent laryngeal nerve runs immediately behind the thyroid and is at risk during thyroidectomy. A unilateral paralysis leaves one cord unable to meet the other, which gives the breathy voice from air escaping and allows aspiration during swallowing, hence the coughing on drinking. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a mass in the upper neck that swells at mealtimes. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A branchial cleft cyst","A metastatic lymph node","A salivary rather than a nodal cause","A thyroid nodule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Swelling that varies with eating implicates a salivary gland, because eating stimulates salivary flow and an obstructed gland distends. Lymph nodes and neoplasms do not change size over minutes, so the timing of the swelling localizes the problem before any imaging is arranged. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman on immunosuppression after transplantation has persistent cervical lymphadenopathy. Bacterial cultures are negative and antibiotics have failed. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Increase the antibiotic spectrum","Reduce the immunosuppression and observe","Send fungal cultures and serology","Start empirical antituberculous therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Immunocompromised patients are particularly susceptible to fungal infection, most commonly Candida, Histoplasma and Aspergillus, and fungal cultures AND serology are required to make the diagnosis. Negative bacterial cultures with antibiotic failure in this host is precisely the setting in which to look. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 41-year-old has had three episodes of room-spinning vertigo over two months, each lasting about two hours, with nausea, a roaring tinnitus and a sense of fullness in the left ear. Hearing fluctuates between attacks. An audiogram is arranged. Which frequencies are expected to be affected earliest?","choices":["All frequencies equally","High frequencies","Low frequencies","Only frequencies above 8000 hertz"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The distension of endolymphatic hydrops is greatest at the cochlear apex, which is the region that codes low frequencies, so the early audiogram shows a low-frequency sensorineural loss that fluctuates. That pattern distinguishes it from almost every other sensorineural cause, which start high. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man is diagnosed with a thyroid carcinoma arising from parafollicular C cells. Beyond his own treatment, the clinician arranges an additional action involving his relatives. What additional action should be taken?","choices":["No family screening, since thyroid cancer is not heritable","Screen family members for Hashimoto thyroiditis","Screen family members for multiple endocrine neoplasia","Screen family members for papillary carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medullary carcinoma arises from the calcitonin-producing C cells and carries a familial association with multiple endocrine neoplasia. Because relatives may carry the same predisposition and can be identified before disease develops, screening the family is part of the management of the index case rather than an optional extra. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man wakes to find he cannot hear from his right ear. There is no pain, discharge or trauma. Otoscopy is normal. Weber lateralizes to the left and Rinne shows air conduction greater than bone conduction bilaterally. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A course of oral antibiotics for presumed otitis media","Microsuction of the canal for presumed wax","Reassurance and review in six weeks","Urgent referral for sudden sensorineural loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal otoscopy with Weber lateralizing AWAY from the affected ear and a normal Rinne pattern in both ears establishes the loss is sensorineural rather than conductive. Sudden sensorineural loss is a syndrome rather than a diagnosis, and it is time-critical: the chance of recovery falls the longer treatment is delayed, so referral is prompt rather than routine. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man with a week of sinusitis symptoms now has swelling and erythema around the right eye, pain on eye movement and double vision. He is febrile. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Ophthalmology referral for the diplopia alone","Oral amoxicillin-clavulanate and review in 48 hours","Topical decongestants and nasal steroid","Urgent imaging and admission for intravenous antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Periorbital swelling with painful restricted eye movement and diplopia means the infection has crossed from the sinus into the orbit, because the ethmoid sinus is separated from it only by the paper-thin lamina papyracea. That is orbital cellulitis, which threatens vision and can spread intracranially, so it needs imaging and intravenous therapy now. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man has chronic unilateral nasal obstruction, snoring, and recurrent nosebleeds from the more open side. Examination shows a markedly deviated septum. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Antihistamines","Long-term intranasal corticosteroid","Nasal packing to reduce the nosebleeds","Septoplasty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A deviated septum narrows one passage and diverts airflow through the other, and that accelerated dry airflow cracks the mucosa on the open side, which is why the nosebleeds come from there rather than from the obstructed side. Only straightening the septum corrects the airflow, so surgery is the definitive treatment when symptoms justify it. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with long-standing Hashimoto thyroiditis develops a rapidly enlarging thyroid mass. Fine needle aspiration is reported as showing a lymphoid infiltrate that cannot be confidently distinguished from her known thyroiditis. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Accept the result as Hashimoto and continue thyroid hormone replacement","Proceed to open biopsy, because aspiration cannot separate lymphoma from Hashimoto","Repeat the fine needle aspiration with more passes","Total thyroidectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Primary thyroid lymphoma is most often a non-Hodgkin B cell tumor arising on a background of Hashimoto thyroiditis, and both conditions fill the gland with lymphocytes. Aspiration cytology cannot reliably tell a reactive infiltrate from a monoclonal one, so tissue architecture is needed, followed by lymphoma staging. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 32-year-old competitive surfer has bilateral, symmetrical, smooth bony swellings narrowing both ear canals. He reports recurrent trapped water and occasional infections. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cerumen impaction","Cholesteatoma","Exostoses","Osteoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Repeated exposure to cold water stimulates new bone formation in the canal walls, which is why the condition is known as surfer's ear and why it is bilateral and symmetrical. The clinical consequence is a narrowed canal that traps water and debris, producing the recurrent otitis externa he describes. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman with rapidly progressive bilateral sensorineural loss is suspected of having autoimmune inner ear disease. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A trial of systemic corticosteroids","Hearing aids alone","Immediate cochlear implantation","Observation with repeat audiometry in a year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Autoimmune inner ear disease is one of the few sensorineural losses that responds to treatment, and the response to systemic corticosteroids serves as both therapy and a diagnostic test, since the condition is otherwise hard to confirm. That responsiveness is the reason it must be considered before a bilateral progressive loss is written off. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman on warfarin has a nosebleed that has stopped with pressure. Her international normalized ratio is 6.2. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Admit for immediate posterior packing","Correct the anticoagulation as well as treating the nose","Stop warfarin permanently","Treat the nose locally and continue warfarin unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Clotting studies are not routine in epistaxis but are indicated in anticoagulated patients, and this result is well above range. Treating only the local bleeding point leaves the systemic reason it bled and the risk of rebleeding or of hemorrhage elsewhere entirely unaddressed. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 3-year-old has fever, sore throat, neck stiffness and refusal to eat for two days. He is now drooling and leaning forward with his neck extended. A lateral neck radiograph shows widening of the prevertebral soft tissue. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Bacterial pharyngitis","Epiglottitis","Peritonsillar abscess","Retropharyngeal abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Suppuration of retropharyngeal nodes after an upper respiratory infection is a disease of children under five, and the widened prevertebral space on a lateral film is the classic radiographic sign. It is a surgical emergency because the space runs from skull base to posterior mediastinum, and mediastinitis carries a 50 percent mortality. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man has nasal crusting, whistling on inspiration and recurrent minor nosebleeds. Examination shows a septal perforation. He has used intranasal steroid for years for allergic rhinitis. What is the most likely cause?","choices":["A previous undiagnosed nasal fracture","Chronic ischemia from a topical agent","Nasal polyps eroding the septum","Untreated allergic rhinitis itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The anterior septum has a thin mucosa over cartilage with no other blood supply, so anything that causes sustained vasoconstriction or mucosal injury there can perforate it. Prolonged intranasal steroid, particularly if sprayed at the septum rather than laterally, and cocaine are the two named causes, and the history has to ask about both. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has had a discharging left ear for four months. It has not responded to three different topical antibiotics. The discharge is now blood-stained and he has developed deep aching pain. Otoscopy shows friable tissue in the canal. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A fourth course of topical antibiotics with a different agent","Aural toilet and observation for six weeks","Biopsy of the canal lesion","Systemic antifungal therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Otitis externa that will not respond to appropriate treatment, together with bloody otorrhea and friable tissue, is the described presentation of canal carcinoma. Only histology distinguishes malignancy from chronic inflammation or granulation, and the treatment pathway is entirely different, so tissue has to be obtained rather than another drop tried. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 71"}]};
